    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Type 2 diabetes (E11.65)</content>. Symptoms easing on metformin. Foot examination normal and retinal photographs show no retinopathy. Urine albumin normal at intake. For a woman with obesity and type 2 diabetes the Standards favour a GLP-1 receptor agonist for both glucose and weight; an SGLT2 inhibitor is reserved for kidney disease, heart failure or established cardiovascular disease, none of which she has. She chose semaglutide after hearing the benefits, the common nausea, the rare pancreatitis and gallbladder problems, and that it must be stopped two months before any planned pregnancy. [S]<list><item>Semaglutide 0.25 mg weekly for four weeks, then 0.5 mg.</item><item>Small meals, stopping when full.</item><item>Continue metformin 1000 mg.</item><item>A1c at about three months.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Obesity, class 2 (E66.812)</content>. She raised the subject of her weight herself and set her own goal, to manage stairs more easily. Weight 91.5 kg, down 1.5 kg. [S]<list><item>Strengthening exercise and protein at each meal to protect muscle and bone as weight falls.</item><item>Weight recorded only with her permission.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Periodontitis (K05.323)</content>. Socket healed; ready for periodontal therapy. [S]<list><item>Scaling and root planing with the dental team in two weeks.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Knee osteoarthritis (M17.0)</content>. Better with exercise and topical diclofenac. [S]<list><item>Continue the exercises; walking goal of 150 minutes a week.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Cardiovascular risk</content>. Diabetes at 48 with LDL 128 mg/dL, low HDL and high triglycerides; the Standards advise a moderate-intensity statin for adults 40 to 75 with diabetes. ALT normal at baseline. [S]<list><item>Atorvastatin 20 mg daily.</item><item>Lipids and ALT at the next review.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Hemoglobin A1c</td><td>Every three months until at goal or after a change in therapy, then every six months</td><td>Due</td><td>7.6% at diagnosis six weeks ago; next at about three months</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Blood pressure (goal below 130/80)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>124/78</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Weight and BMI, recorded with her permission</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>91.5 kg, BMI 35.7</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Metformin: dose, tolerance and sick-day rules</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>1000 mg tolerated; sick-day rules reviewed</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>GLP-1 receptor agonist: dose, nausea, warning signs</td><td>At start and every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Semaglutide 0.25 mg weekly started; nausea, pancreatitis, gallbladder and pregnancy advice given</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Hypoglycemia review</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Metformin and semaglutide do not cause hypoglycemia on their own</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Comprehensive foot examination</td><td>At diagnosis, then yearly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Normal</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Diabetic eye screening</td><td>At diagnosis, then every one to two years</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Retinal photographs: no retinopathy</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio and eGFR</td><td>At diagnosis, then yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>UACR 18 mg/g and eGFR 103 at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Lipid panel and ALT on a statin</td><td>Baseline, then 4 to 12 weeks after starting a statin</td><td>Due</td><td>Atorvastatin started today; ALT 31 U/L at baseline; lipids and ALT at the next review</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Diabetes self-management education and nutrition therapy</td><td>At diagnosis, then yearly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Two of four sessions attended</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Dental and periodontal care</td><td>At diagnosis, then periodontal maintenance every three months</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Socket healed; scaling and root planing booked</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Knee pain and function</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Better with exercise and gel</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Dental infection drained and the tooth removed within 24 hours, with no spread of infection. Outcome by 2026-04-15: Drained and extracted the next day; no spread [S]</item><item>Hemoglobin A1c below 7% within four months. In progress; target 2026-07-21 [S]</item><item>LDL cholesterol below 100 mg/dL on a statin. In progress; target 2026-07-21 [S]</item><item>Periodontal therapy completed and three-monthly maintenance arranged. In progress; target 2026-07-21 [S]</item><item>Climb stairs at the building sites with less knee pain, and walk 150 minutes a week. In progress; target 2026-07-21 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Standards of Care in Diabetes—2026 (Section 2, Diagnosis and Classification of Diabetes; Section 9, Pharmacologic Approaches to Glycemic Treatment) (2026); Obesity and Weight Management for the Prevention and Treatment of Diabetes: Standards of Care in Diabetes—2026 (Section 8) (2026); Cardiovascular Disease and Risk Management: Standards of Care in Diabetes—2026 (Section 10) (2026); Scientific evidence on the links between periodontal diseases and diabetes: Consensus report and guidelines of the joint workshop on periodontal diseases and diabetes by the International Diabetes Federation and the European Federation of Periodontology (2018); 2019 American College of Rheumatology/Arthritis Foundation Guideline for the Management of Osteoarthritis of the Hand, Hip, and Knee (2019); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, diabetes type 2, rheumatoid arthritis and osteoarthritis, and hyperlipidemia monographs (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Preventive visit as booked; fourteen-week review with A1c, lipids and ALT. Next scheduled visit 2026-06-30: periodic adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

